---------------------------------------------------------------
1. TYPES OF USERS IN LINUX
---------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------+------------+--------------------------------+
| User Type      | UID Range  | Description                    |
+----------------+------------+--------------------------------+
| Root User      | 0          | Full system privileges         |
| System Users   | 1 - 999    | Service accounts, no login     |
| Regular Users  | 1000+      | Human users (e.g., dinesh)     |
+----------------+------------+--------------------------------+

Important Files:
  /etc/passwd  --> Stores username, UID, home dir, shell
                   [x] in password field = password is in shadow
  /etc/shadow  --> Stores actual hashed passwords (root only)

---------------------------------------------------------------
2. USER MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
---------------------------------------------------------------

ADD USER:
  useradd username

SET PASSWORD:
  passwd username

DELETE USER:
  userdel username
  userdel -r username        # Also removes home directory

INTERACTIVE USER CREATION (Debian/Ubuntu only):
  adduser username           # Asks questions step by step

VIEW ALL USERS:
  getent passwd

---------------------------------------------------------------
3. USER MODIFICATION — usermod FLAGS
---------------------------------------------------------------

FLAG        USAGE                              NOTES
-d -m       Change home dir & move data        See struggle note below
-l          Rename username                    Does NOT rename home dir
-c          Add comment (full name, address)
-u          Change UID
-g          Change primary group               Only 1 primary group allowed
-aG         Add to secondary group             Always use -aG (append)
-G          Assign secondary group             WARNING: Removes from all other groups!
-e          Set account expiry (YYYY-MM-DD)
-L          Lock user account                  Adds ! in /etc/shadow
-U          Unlock user account                Only root can lock/unlock
-s          Set user shell                     /sbin/nologin = disable login
-r          Create system account


EXAMPLES:
  usermod -d /new/path/username -m username      # Change home dir
  usermod -l newname oldname                     # Rename user
  usermod -aG groupname username                 # Add to secondary group
  usermod -L username                            # Lock account
  usermod -U username                            # Unlock account
  usermod -s /sbin/nologin username              # Disable login shell
  usermod -e 2025-12-31 username                 # Set expiry date
  usermod -c "Full Name" username                # Add comment

---------------------------------------------------------------
4. HANDS-ON STRUGGLE & FIX (Real Learning!)
---------------------------------------------------------------

PROBLEM:
  I created a user with a home directory, then tried to change
  the home directory using:

    usermod -d /new-dir/username -m username

  Result --> /etc/passwd showed the new path correctly BUT
             the new directory had no user subfolder inside
             and no data was moved anywhere.

ROOT CAUSE:
  The target directory must already exist before running
  the usermod command. The -m flag only MOVES data,
  it does not CREATE the destination directory.

FIX — Method 1 (Recommended):
  Step 1: mkdir -p /new-dir/username
  Step 2: usermod -d /new-dir/username -m username

FIX — Method 2 (Manual):
  Step 1: mkdir -p /new-dir/username
  Step 2: chown username:groupname /new-dir/username
  Step 3: cp -r /old-home/* /new-dir/username/

---------------------------------------------------------------
5. SECURITY LEARNING — Lock / Shell Disable
---------------------------------------------------------------

LOCK ACCOUNT:
  usermod -L username
  --> Adds ! before hashed password in /etc/shadow
  --> Linux still prompts for password on login (security behavior)
  --> Correct password is REJECTED due to lock
  --> sudo users can still switch to the account (no password needed)
  --> Very useful during security breach — instant account lock!

UNLOCK ACCOUNT:
  usermod -U username
  --> Only root user can lock/unlock

DISABLE LOGIN SHELL:
  usermod -s /sbin/nologin username
  --> Nobody can login to this account via terminal
  --> Powerful Linux security practice for service accounts

---------------------------------------------------------------
6. GROUP MANAGEMENT
---------------------------------------------------------------

Important File:
  /etc/group  --> Stores group name, password(x), GID, members

TYPES OF GROUPS:
  Primary Group   --> Default group of user
                      Files created by user are owned by this group
  Secondary Group --> Additional groups for extra access permissions
                      A user can belong to many secondary groups

GROUP COMMANDS:

  groupadd groupname              # Add new group
  groupadd -g 1500 groupname      # Add group with custom GID
  groupadd -r groupname           # Create system group (GID < 1000)
  groupmod -n newname oldname     # Rename group
  groupmod -g new-gid groupname   # Change GID
  groupdel groupname              # Delete group

  WARNING: Cannot delete a user's primary group directly.
           Use: sudo groupdel -f groupname  (force delete)

---------------------------------------------------------------
7. GROUP MEMBERSHIP — gpasswd
---------------------------------------------------------------

  gpasswd -a username groupname            # Add user to group
  gpasswd -d username groupname            # Remove user from group
  gpasswd -M user1,user2,user3 groupname   # Add multiple users at once

---------------------------------------------------------------
8. USEFUL UTILITY COMMANDS
---------------------------------------------------------------

  COMMAND                  PURPOSE
  ----------------------   ------------------------------------
  id username              Show UID, GID, primary & secondary groups
  who                      Show currently logged-in users
  w                        Detailed logged-in user info
  groups username          Show all groups a user belongs to
  getent passwd            View all users (/etc/passwd entries)
  getent group             View all groups (/etc/group entries)

---------------------------------------------------------------
9. KEY TAKEAWAYS FROM DAY 05
---------------------------------------------------------------

  [1] Always use -aG (not just -G) when adding to secondary groups.
      -G alone removes user from all other groups!

  [2] Create the target directory BEFORE changing home dir with usermod.

  [3] Locking an account (-L) adds ! in /etc/shadow.
      Linux still asks for password but rejects it. sudo users unaffected.

  [4] /sbin/nologin shell is a simple but powerful security measure.

  [5] /etc/group is the correct file (NOT /etc/groups).

  [6] adduser (Debian/Ubuntu) is interactive. useradd is universal.

  [7] gpasswd -M replaces ALL members — use carefully!

